    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "The primary cause of changing seasons on Earth is the tilt of its rotational axis relative to its orbital plane, combined with its revolution around the Sun.",
      "id": "79phgzt"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Earth's seasons are caused by its 23.5-degree axial tilt combined with its revolution around the Sun.",
        "Solstices mark the extreme position of the Sun's direct rays at the Tropic of Cancer (June 21) and Tropic of Capricorn (December 22).",
        "Equinoxes occur when direct sunlight falls directly on the Equator, resulting in equal day and night durations worldwide.",
        "Orbital distance variations (Perihelion and Aphelion) do not cause seasonal changes; axial orientation is the primary driver."
      ],
      "id": "0ey1u81"
    },
